  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 149 PM Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Dry weather and a little cooler this week.\n\nA well-defined pressure trough is located across central NC attm, \nbut the actual cold front is still well to our NW beginning to \napproach the Ohio Valley.  That front will finally cross central NC \ntomorrow, first a wind shift to the north early in the day followed \nby the actual airmass change later in the evening or Monday night. \nSo we'll still have one more hot day tomorrow with highs around 90 \nbefore the airmass changes.  While a few models suggest a few isold \nshowers could occur sometime during the daytime Monday associated \nwith the front, the bulk of the NWP guidance keeps pops <15%, so for \nnow will keep the forecast dry for Monday.  \n\nOtherwise, a cooler and drier airmass will begin to move into \ncentral NC Monday night as high pressure begins to build southward \ninto our area.  High pressure and dry, more seasonable weather will \npersist through at least the mid-week period. Thereafter, \nstatistical guidance suggests rain chances increasing for the late-\nweek and next weekend periods, but the pattern at that time still \nshows an upper ridge over our region, so perhaps any chances for \nrain later in the forecast period will be below climo.",
